  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Mass is a fundamental physical quantity representing the amount of matter in an object and its resistance to acceleration (inertia). Measured in kilograms (kg) via balances or force-based scales, it remains constant regardless of location or gravity, unlike weight. Key types include inertial mass (resistance to velocity change) and gravitational mass (strength of gravitational attraction). Key Aspects of Mass Formula & Calculation: While mass is often fundamental, it can be derived in several ways: Newton's Second Law: 𝑚=𝐹𝑎 (Force divided by acceleration). Density Formula: 𝑚=𝜌×𝑉 (Density ρrho𝜌 times Volume Vcap V𝑉). Weight Relation: 𝑚=𝑊𝑔 (Weight divided by gravitational acceleration). Units of Mass: The SI base unit is the kilogram (kg). Other common units include grams (g), milligrams (mg), and pounds (lb) (though pounds are often used for weight, they can represent mass in certain systems). How Mass is Measured: Mass is measured using instruments like balance scales (comparing against known masses) or spring scales (measuring gravitational force which is then used to infer mass). Types of Mass: Inertial Mass: Measures an object's resistance to acceleration when a force is applied. Gravitational Mass: Determines the strength of the gravitational force exerted by or on the body. These two are considered equal, a concept key to the theory of relativity. Role in Physics: Mass dictates how much an object resists changes in motion and dictates the magnitude of gravitational attraction (e.g., Earth's gravity on a person). It is a measure of the total material content.",
